From 99dd75c7d4ef4de841b2f8cf59fbf470a102f624 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andy1210 <andy1210@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] backend/drm: a modeset is not a failed cursor scan-out

wlroots attaches an empty buffer to the output for a modeset, and the liftoff
interface then refuses the whole commit if libliftoff did not give the cursor
layer a plane. For an ordinary frame that is the right call - there is no other
way to draw a cursor on this path. For a modeset it is not: the allocation
describes a configuration that is about to change, over a buffer that holds
nothing, and the cursor is re-evaluated on the first real frame either way.

So a resolution change fails outright. Measured on a Raspberry Pi 5 with
WLR_DRM_FORCE_LIBLIFTOFF=1: 3840x2160 -> 1920x1080 fails every time, with
"Failed to scan-out cursor plane" as the only clue and "failed to apply
configuration" reaching whoever asked. The same transition succeeds with
libliftoff off, and on the atomic interface.
---
 backend/drm/libliftoff.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/backend/drm/libliftoff.c b/backend/drm/libliftoff.c
index def4968..4b26d5a 100644
--- a/backend/drm/libliftoff.c
+++ b/backend/drm/libliftoff.c
@@ -558,7 +558,13 @@ static bool commit(struct wlr_drm_backend *drm,
 			goto out;
 		}
 
-		if (crtc->cursor &&
+		/*
+		 * Not on a modeset: the primary buffer is the empty one attached for it,
+		 * the allocation describes a configuration that is about to change, and
+		 * the cursor is re-evaluated on the first real frame. Refusing here fails
+		 * the mode change itself.
+		 */
+		if (!state->modeset && crtc->cursor &&
 				liftoff_layer_needs_composition(crtc->cursor->liftoff_layer)) {
 			wlr_drm_conn_log(conn, WLR_DEBUG, "Failed to scan-out cursor plane");
 			goto out;
